# Palm: one trunk (or a clump of them) under a spiral crown of large
# fronds, after the fan and feather palms, cycads and tree ferns. Each year
# a crown adds fronds_per_year fronds on a golden-angle spiral, each a
# little above the last, and its trunk grows trunk_rate metres a year once
# the palm has built its girth underground (stemless_years: until then
# the crown sits on the ground and the trunk grows only a share of its
# rate, rising smoothly to all of it). A palm's trunk keeps its girth
# (trunk_radius): palms do not thicken with age.
#
# A frond is a petiole (petiole of its length) and a rachis of `pieces`
# pieces:
#
# - a feather frond (fan 0) carries a pair of leaflets at each half piece,
#   longest at leaflet_peak along the rachis, raised leaflet_lift degrees
#   into a V;
# - a fan frond (fan 1) carries, at the petiole's tip, `rays` segments
#   spread over fan_spread degrees, each a blade fan_length of the frond
#   long, its tip bent down by the blade's own bend; a costapalmate fan
#   (as in the cabbage palm) has a short rachis (costa) the rays follow.
#
# A frond unrolls as a fern's does (`polypodiidae`): emerging at a phase e of the
# year, it has unrolled the share u = clamp((age + season - e) / unroll,
# 0, 1) at the keyframe, each piece beyond u coiling back by up to `curl`
# degrees (set curl 0 for a palm, whose spear opens without coiling). It
# opens from young_angle off the vertical toward old_angle as it ages and
# sags under a tropism toward the ground of elasticity
# droop * min(a, droop_years), so the oldest living fronds hang lowest.
# After frond_life years a frond dies: it falls (skirt 0) or hangs dead
# against the trunk at dead_angle (skirt 1, the petticoat of a fan palm or
# a tree fern) for dead_life years. A fallen frond may leave its boot, the
# split base of its petiole, on the trunk for boot_life years (boots 1).
#
# A crown may bear a cone at its centre (crown_cone > 0, a cycad) and send
# up suckers from its foot like a rosette's pups: from sucker_age, with the
# chance sucker_rate a year, at most `suckers` of them, sucker_distance
# out. A leaning trunk (lean, upturn) turns back toward the sky; a creeping
# palm (lean near 90) runs along the ground with its crown raised.
#
# Revision 2 (spec rework M7): each frond is drawn within a `frond`
# structure: a feather frond's leaflets are leaflets of it, and a fan's
# rays, the dead frond hanging and the boot are pieces of it.
#
# Revision 3 (spec rework P6): the first year the plant is adult, from
# which it bears a cone, is adult_age, the name the programs that measure it
# by the plant's age share, which the rule at the seed plants sets from
# a taxon's juvenile_period_years. It replaces cone_age, with the same
# default.
#
# Revision 4 (growth plan G2.3a): a starting base with no values. Each
# parameter given as a number is `param name;`, and the lineage programs
# that extend it (`arecaceae`, `cyatheaceae`, `cycadaceae`) give the
# values their species leave unset, as this program's defaults were until
# then.
#
# Revision 5 (plants fitted to their place): a frond that sags to the
# ground lies on it, `rest_gap` above the substrate (substrate@1's, or
# level soil), instead of passing into it.
#
# Revision 6 (leaf life): fronds, dead fronds, boots and the crown's cone
# live as long as their parameters say, no longer one year more. What a
# step makes is born at the step's end, so a rule that ends a part by its
# age counts the step it is in (`age + dt >=`).
#
# Revision 7 (palms from their facts): a trunk's girth follows its
# life, a crownshaft stands under the crown, feather fronds' leaflets
# stand in two planes, a fan's segments arch down to its edges, and old
# fronds hang. Where a segment of trunk forms when its crown is a years
# old, its radius is trunk_radius times 1 + flare * exp(-a' / flare_years)
# (a' the years since the stemless ones: the swollen foot of a royal or a
# fan palm) + bulge * exp(-((a - bulge_age) / bulge_years)^2) (the royal
# palm's belly, or a bottle palm's). A crownshaft (crownshaft > 0, the
# royal palm's green column of leaf sheaths) is drawn as shaft_cards
# sheaths round the trunk's top, crownshaft long, each year's living as
# long as its fronds, and the fronds rise from its top. A feather frond's
# leaflets rise leaflet_lift ± leaflet_scatter degrees, the two halves of
# each piece in turn, so a plumose frond's stand in two planes. A fan's
# rays droop by up to fan_arch degrees toward its edges, as a costapalmate
# fan arches over its rib. A frond turns hang degrees further from the
# vertical over its life, so the oldest hang below the crown. Each at 0
# draws as revision 6 did.
#
# Revision 8 (a crownshaft's sheaths are its fronds'): each frond draws
# its own sheath, within its `frond` structure, as a palm's leaf sheath is
# the base of its leaf: max(1, round(shaft_cards / fronds_per_year))
# cards round the crownshaft's core, from the frond's foot down the
# crownshaft's length, living and dying with the frond. Until revision 7
# a year's sheaths stood round the trunk's top within no structure, which
# the organ check refuses for a piece.
#
# Revision 9 (cycads, palms and tree ferns told apart; Whitelock 2002,
# Dransfield et al. 2008, Hoshizaki and Moran 2001, Kaplan 2022):
#
# - A trunk may thicken below as it ages, as a cycad's does and as a tree
#   fern's mantle of roots does: a segment formed with radius r is
#   r (1 + thicken (1 - exp(-a / thicken_years))) when a years old, so
#   the oldest, lowest part is widest. A palm's stays as it formed
#   (thicken 0).
# - Leaves may come in flushes: every flush_interval years a crown puts out
#   all of a flush's fronds_per_year fronds at once (a cycad's crown, with
#   emerge_spread small), and none between. A flush may open with a ring
#   of `cataphylls` scale leaves, cataphyll_length long, pressed against
#   the trunk cataphyll_angle degrees out, which stay cataphyll_life
#   years: with the leaf stubs (boots) they band a cycad's trunk, one band
#   a flush.
# - A boot stands boot_angle degrees from the trunk and starts at the
#   trunk's surface beside its frond's foot, no longer inside it.
# - A frond's leaflets open by their place along it: a leaflet at share x
#   of the rachis has opened g = clamp(2 u - x, 0, 1) when the frond has
#   unrolled the share u, so the lowest open first. While opening it is
#   leaflet_young of its size (a Cycas leaflet still coiled) and lies
#   leaflet_fold of the way from its angle to along the rachis (a palm's
#   spear, a zamiad's folded leaflets).
# - A coiled piece is shorter by coil_short of its length, so a tree
#   fern's crozier is a tight coil.
# - A feather frond's leaflets may stand in groups of leaflet_groups, the
#   lifts of a group spread from +leaflet_scatter to -leaflet_scatter, so a
#   plumose palm's leaflets stand in several planes (2 alternates them,
#   as before).
#
# - The trunk between one frond and the next is as thick as the trunk
#   below it (until now it was trunk_radius, so a swollen or flared
#   trunk was notched at each frond).
# - A crownshaft's sheath wraps it: each frond's sheath is a ring of
#   shaft_cards cards (until now shaft_cards / fronds_per_year) from the
#   core's surface at the frond's foot, lying against it and leaning out
#   1.2 degrees, so the living fronds' sheaths make a closed green column
#   a little wider at its foot. Until now each card stood edgewise out
#   from the core, a fin.
#
# thicken, cataphylls, leaflet_fold and coil_short 0, flush_interval 1,
# leaflet_young 0.3, leaflet_groups 2 and boot_angle 60 draw as revision 8
# did, but for boots and the leaflets' order of opening.
#
# Revision 10 (Phoenix's spines and leaf sheaths):
# - The lowest `acanthophylls` pairs of leaflets are spines (Phoenix's
#   acanthophylls; Dransfield et al. 2008, Genera Palmarum p. 242),
#   `spine_length` long at the highest and 0.4 of that at the lowest,
#   standing as the leaflets do.
# - Without a crownshaft, a frond's sheath may clasp the stem
#   `sheath_length` below its foot (`Sh`, as a crownshaft's does), so a
#   crown's living leaf bases hide the stem's top (the California fan
#   palm's chestnut-brown sheaths, Sargent 1922 p. 104).
# acanthophylls and sheath_length 0 draw as revision 9.
#
# Revision 11 (visual forms tree-fern croziers): a circinate frond comes
# up as a fern's crozier does (`polypodiidae` revisions 11 to 13). Where
# coil_turns is above 0, the rachis beyond the unrolled share winds
# coil_turns turns in all, each coiled piece shorter the nearer the tip
# (exp(-coil_tight d) of coil_size of its length, d its distance beyond
# the share unrolled), drawn as four short segments, so the crozier is a
# tight, round, inward spiral free of the frond's sag, not a hook of
# `curl`. A coiled leaflet is small (coil_pinna of its size at the
# outer turn, less inward) and pressed to the coil, and beside it the
# leaflet is itself rolled, a short coil as long as the coiled pieces
# shrink it and coil_roll of that thick, never thicker than the rachis,
# as each pinna coils as its frond does (Kaplan 2022, p. 352, 454); both
# open by their place as the frond unrolls from its foot. Every frond is
# drawn young (`young`) until it has unrolled, fading over as long again,
# so a form whose appearance gives a young_axis colour draws its croziers
# in it. coil_turns 0 draws as revision 10 did.
# Revision 12 (cones by sex; Whitelock 2002, p. 18-21): a dioecious
# species' plants (dioecious 1, from the sexual system its taxa state) are
# male, their own draw `sex` below a half, or female. In a coning year, a
# flush year from adult_age on, every cone_interval years (those ending on
# a multiple of it, so a keyframe at one shows its cones), a crown bears:
#
# - on a male plant, male_cones pollen cones (`cone_male`, shading with
#   male_cone_area), male_cone long, each on a stalk male_peduncle long,
#   leaning male_lean degrees from the vertical, living male_cone_life
#   years;
# - on a female plant, or one bearing both sexes, a crown of
#   `sporophylls` megasporophylls round the stem's tip, as a female Cycas
#   bears in place of a cone (`sporophyll`, sporophyll_length long, with
#   `ovules` seeds along its stalk, hanging from it), or else `cones` seed
#   cones (`cone`, and `cone_open` as it breaks up, shading with
#   open_cone_area), crown_cone long, on stalks `peduncle` long; both live
#   cone_life years.
#
# Several cones stand in a ring cone_offset of the trunk's radius out
# from the crown's centre (at least four tenths, so they stand apart),
# among the leaf bases, and lean outward; a
# crown holds up to 64 seed or pollen cones and 128 sporophylls. A
# keyframe draws a crown of sporophylls as it is at the end of that year
# of its life, the share of its life q = clamp((age + dt) / cone_life, 0,
# 1) behind it: a sporophyll turns from sporophyll_angle toward
# sporophyll_ripe (a C. thouarsii's hang when their seed is ripe), its
# seeds swelling to seed_length. A seed cone is drawn as it is at the
# keyframe's season, q = clamp((age + season) / cone_life, 0, 1): it
# leans from cone_lean toward ripe_lean, and stays closed over its seeds
# until, in the last tenth of its life, it breaks up, showing cone_seeds
# seeds round its surface, cone_width across. The stem grows on
# through a crown of sporophylls, as a female Cycas's does. With
# crown_cone, male_cone and sporophylls 0 a palm bears none, as before.
#
# Revision 13 (fan palms' blades, petiole teeth, skirts and fishtail
# palms; Dransfield et al. 2008, Genera Palmarum p. 16-17, 216, 285, 297;
# Sargent 1922, p. 101-105):
#
# - A fan's segments are free only over their outer split_depth of their
#   length; inside that the blade is one piece, the palman, drawn as
#   wedges each palman_angle degrees wide from the fan's centre to where
#   the segments part. The free part of each segment turns down tip_droop
#   degrees where it parts as the frond opens (Washingtonia's pendulous
#   segments, Sabal's drooping ones), the palman staying as it was.
# - A fan's segments may bear `filaments`, threads hanging from the blade:
#   a segment's first in the sinus between it and the next, the rest along
#   its free margin to its tip, each filament_length of the segment's free
#   part; a share below one puts a thread on that share of the segments.
# - A petiole's margins may bear teeth, tooth_length long, tooth_spacing
#   apart on average, standing tooth_angle degrees from the petiole toward
#   the blade (over 90, leaning back toward the stem, as curved and hooked
#   teeth do), the last tooth_tip of the first's size and the teeth sparser
#   toward the blade by as much; the petiole is then drawn in a piece
#   from each pair of teeth to the next.
# - A dead frond hangs from the trunk's surface beside its foot, no longer
#   from inside the trunk, at dead_angle from the vertical (the world's,
#   not the trunk's), so the dead fronds lie against the trunk, petiole
#   up and blade down, as a thatch; it first stands out at its last
#   living angle and falls to dead_angle over dead_fold_years, so the
#   thatch meets the living crown.
# - A twice-pinnate frond (pinna_pairs > 0, Caryota's) bears pinna_pairs
#   pairs of pinnae spread evenly along its rachis in place of its
#   leaflets, each a rachis as long as the leaflet would be, sized along
#   the frond as the leaflets are, with pinna_leaflets leaflets
#   (`pinnule`) a side and one at its tip, pinnule_length of the pinna
#   long and shorter toward its tip, at pinnule_angle to it.
#
# split_depth 1, tip_droop, filaments, tooth_length and pinna_pairs 0 draw
# as revision 12 did, but for the dead fronds.
#
# Revision 14 (a year's fronds up the year's trunk): a frond stands on
# the stem where the stem had grown to when it emerged. A year's fronds
# emerge over emerge_spread of the year, the i-th of the year's k at
# phase emerge_spread (i + x) / k for a draw x from 0 to 1 (until now any
# phase from 0 to emerge_spread, in any order up the stem), and that
# share of the year's growth of trunk lies among them, each frond
# trunk_rate · emerge_spread / k above the last, so a palm's leaf bases,
# boots and scars wind evenly up its trunk (Dransfield et al. 2008,
# Genera Palmarum p. 10: each leaf leaves one node, so the spacing of the
# rings is a year's growth over the year's leaves; p. 242, a Phoenix stem
# clothed in spirally arranged leaf bases). Until now the
# whole year's fronds stood within internode of each other at the
# year's top, so the trunk between was bare and its boots stood in bands.
# A cycad's flush (emerge_spread near 0) still stands as one band, and a
# crownshaft's fronds still stand at its top.
#
# Revision 15 (worn stubs and the root mass):
# - A boot wears shorter as it ages: a years old it is
#   boot_length (1 - boot_wear (1 - exp(-a / boot_wear_years))) long,
#   times a draw from 1 ± boot_spread of its own, so an old trunk's lower
#   stubs are short and its outline ragged. A Phoenix trunk is covered
#   with leaf-sheath bases when young and later becomes smooth (Flora of
#   Tropical East Africa, via Colplanta), the pattern of scars showing
#   where the bases have gone (University of Arizona Campus Arboretum).
# - The foot puts out `roots`: adventitious roots from the root
#   initiation zone at the trunk's base, root_rate a year from root_age,
#   from heights up to root_zone, the zone rising over root_zone_years.
#   Each runs from the trunk's surface root_angle degrees from straight
#   down to the ground; a share 1 - root_reach of them are arrested in
#   the air, a short stub (University of Florida IFAS EP344: all palm
#   roots are adventitious; above-ground root initials stop growing in
#   dry air; on some Phoenix the visible zone reaches several feet up the
#   trunk).
#
# - An open sheath (`sheath_wedge` 1, Phoenix's: Dransfield et al. 2008,
#   Genera Palmarum p. 13) splits down the side away from its petiole and
#   does not close round the stem, so it is drawn as one broad wedge along
#   the petiole's base, sheath_length past the stem's surface, in place
#   of a ring of cards hanging below the frond's foot. The ring's cards
#   ended in a flat-topped tube at the crown's centre.
#
# boot_wear, boot_spread, roots and sheath_wedge 0 draw as revision 14.
#
# One step is one year. Angles are degrees; lengths are metres.

lsystem palm 15;

# The plant.
param vigour_spread;          # a palm grows exp(gauss() * vigour_spread) times as fast
param lean;                   # most degrees the trunk leans at its foot
param upturn;                 # how strongly a leaning trunk turns back up, radians a metre
# The trunk.
param trunk_rate;             # metres a year once established
param trunk_radius;
param stemless_years;         # years before the trunk rises at its full rate
param internode;              # trunk between one frond and the next
param thicken;                # how much a trunk segment thickens with age, a share of its radius
param thicken_years;          # years over which it does
param flare;                  # the foot's extra girth, a share of trunk_radius
param flare_years;            # years over which the foot's flare narrows to the trunk
param bulge;                  # a belly's extra girth, a share of trunk_radius
param bulge_age;              # the crown's age when the belly's widest part forms
param bulge_years;            # how long the belly lasts, years either side
param crownshaft;             # a crownshaft's length, metres; 0 none
param shaft_cards;            # cards each frond's sheath wraps the crownshaft with
param sheath_area;            # leaf area of a sheath 1 m long, m²
param sheath_length;          # without a crownshaft, how far a frond's sheath clasps the stem below its foot, metres; 0 none
param sheath_wedge;           # 1: an open sheath, drawn as one broad wedge along the petiole's base in place of cards round the stem
# Fronds.
param fronds_per_year;        # fronds a year, or a flush's
param flush_interval;         # years from one flush to the next; 1 every year
param cataphylls;             # scale leaves round the crown at each flush; 0 none
param cataphyll_length;
param cataphyll_angle;        # degrees a scale leaf stands out from the trunk
param cataphyll_life;         # years a scale leaf stays
param cataphyll_area;         # leaf area of a scale leaf 1 m long, m²
param frond_life;             # years a frond lives
param frond_length;           # a full-size frond, petiole included
param frond_seed;             # a seedling's fronds, as a share of full size
param size_years;             # fronds reach full size over about this many years
param frond_spread;           # fronds are l (1 ± frond_spread) long
param divergence;             # roll from one frond to the next
param young_angle;            # angle of a new frond to the vertical
param old_angle;              # the angle an old frond opens to
param open_years;             # how fast a frond opens, years
param angle_spread;
param petiole;                # share of the frond's length that is petiole
param petiole_radius;         # its radius
param pieces;                 # pieces of the rachis
# Feather fronds.
param fan;                    # 1: a fan frond
param leaflet_length;         # the longest leaflet, as a share of the frond's length
param leaflet_peak;           # where it sits along the rachis, 0 to 1
param leaflet_base;           # the lowest leaflet, as a share of the longest
param leaflet_tip;            # the topmost leaflet, as a share of the longest
param leaflet_angle;          # angle of a leaflet to the rachis
param leaflet_lift;           # degrees the leaflets rise into a V
param leaflet_scatter;        # degrees the two halves of a piece's leaflets stand above and below it
param leaflet_area;           # leaf area of a leaflet 1 m long, m²
param leaflet_young;          # a leaflet's size while it opens, as a share of its own
param leaflet_fold;           # how far toward the rachis a leaflet lies while it opens, 0 to 1
param leaflet_groups;         # leaflets a group, their lifts spread over ±leaflet_scatter
param acanthophylls;          # lowest leaflet pairs drawn as spines (Phoenix); 0 none
param spine_length;           # the longest such spine, m
param spine_area;             # leaf area of a spine 1 m long, m²
# Fan fronds.
param rays;                   # segments of a fan
param fan_spread;             # degrees the segments spread over
param fan_length;             # a segment, as a share of the frond's length
param fan_cup;                # degrees the fan's segments rise into a shallow cup
param costa;                  # share of the frond's length along which the rays rise from a rib
param fan_arch;               # degrees a fan's edge rays droop below its middle
param ray_area;               # leaf area of a segment 1 m long, m²
param split_depth;            # share of a segment's length, from its tip, free of its neighbours; 1 to the fan's centre
param palman_angle;           # degrees of the fan's unsplit middle one palman card covers
param palman_area;            # leaf area of a palman card 1 m long, m²
param tip_droop;              # degrees a segment's free part turns down where it parts from the palman
param filaments;              # threads a segment bears, the first in its sinus; 0 none
param filament_length;        # a thread, as a share of its segment's free part
param filament_area;
# Petiole teeth.
param tooth_length;           # the longest tooth on a petiole's margins, m; 0 none
param tooth_spacing;          # metres from one tooth to the next, on average
param tooth_angle;            # degrees a tooth stands from the petiole toward the blade
param tooth_tip;              # the last tooth's size as a share of the first's
param tooth_area;
# Twice-pinnate fronds.
param pinna_pairs;            # pairs of pinnae in place of the leaflets; 0 once pinnate
param pinna_leaflets;         # leaflets each side of a pinna's rachis
param pinnule_length;         # a pinna's longest leaflet, as a share of the pinna
param pinnule_angle;          # angle of a pinna's leaflets to its rachis
param pinnule_area;
# Unrolling, sagging and dying.
param season;                 # share of the year at the keyframe
param emerge_spread;          # fronds emerge over this share of the year
param unroll;                 # share of a year a frond takes to open
param curl;                   # degrees each coiled piece turns back
param coil_short;             # share by which a coiled piece is shorter
param coil_turns;             # turns of a circinate frond's rachis coiled in its crozier; 0 coils by curl and coil_short instead
param coil_size;              # a coiled piece's length at the crozier's outer turn, as a share of its open length
param coil_tight;             # how fast the coiled pieces shorten toward the tip: exp(-coil_tight d)
param coil_pinna;             # a coiled leaflet at the crozier's outer turn, as a share of its open size
param coil_roll;              # a rolled leaflet's thickness, a share of its rolled length
param droop;                  # sag per year of a frond's age
param droop_years;
param hang;                   # degrees a frond turns further from the vertical over its life
param rest_gap;               # a frond that reaches the ground lies this far above it
param skirt;                  # 1: dead fronds hang
param dead_angle;             # angle of a hanging dead frond to the vertical
param dead_life;              # years a dead frond hangs
param dead_fold_years;        # years a dead frond takes to fall from its living angle to dead_angle
param dead_area;              # leaf area of a dead frond 1 m long
param boots;                  # 1: a fallen frond leaves its boot
param boot_life;              # years a boot stays
param boot_length;            # a boot, metres
param boot_angle;             # degrees a boot stands from the trunk
param boot_area;
param boot_wear;              # share of a boot's length it wears away as it ages
param boot_wear_years;        # years over which it does
param boot_spread;            # boots are l (1 ± boot_spread) long
param roots;                  # roots the foot puts out above ground; 0 none
param root_rate;              # roots a year
param root_age;               # the year the first shows
param root_zone;              # highest a root leaves the trunk, metres
param root_zone_years;        # years over which the root zone rises to root_zone
param root_angle;             # degrees a root runs from straight down
param root_radius;
param root_reach;             # share of the roots that reach the ground; the rest stop short in the air
# Cones by sex.
param dioecious;              # 1: each plant is male or female; 0: every plant bears both
param sex;                    # this plant's own draw, 0 to 1: below a half, a dioecious plant is male
param adult_age;              # the first year the plant is adult and bears cones
param cone_interval;          # years from one coning to the next
param crown_cone;             # a seed cone's length; 0 none
param cones;                  # seed cones a coning crown bears
param cone_life;              # years a seed cone, or a crown of sporophylls, stands
param cone_width;             # a seed cone across, where its seeds show as it breaks up
param peduncle;               # a seed cone's stalk, metres
param peduncle_radius;        # a cone's stalk's radius
param cone_offset;            # how far out from the crown's centre cones stand, a share of the trunk's radius
param cone_lean;              # degrees a young seed cone leans from the vertical
param ripe_lean;              # degrees a ripe one does
param cone_seeds;             # seeds a seed cone breaking up shows; 0 none
param seed_length;            # a ripe seed
param male_cone;              # a pollen cone's length; 0 none
param male_cones;             # pollen cones a coning male crown bears
param male_cone_life;         # years a pollen cone stands
param male_peduncle;          # a pollen cone's stalk, metres
param male_lean;              # degrees a pollen cone leans from the vertical
param sporophylls;            # megasporophylls a female crown bears in place of a cone; 0 a cone
param sporophyll_length;
param sporophyll_angle;       # degrees a young sporophyll stands from the vertical
param sporophyll_ripe;        # degrees a ripe one does: over 90 hangs
param ovules;                 # seeds along each sporophyll
param cone_area;
param male_cone_area;
param open_cone_area;
param sporophyll_area;
param seed_area;
# Suckers.
param suckers;
param sucker_age;
param sucker_rate;
param sucker_distance;

module Y(v, n);               # a crown: vigour, suckers so far
module Fc(i, m, c, r, d, k);  # fronds i to i + m - 1 of this year's k, on a crown c years old and a trunk r in radius, each d further up than the last
module Fr(s, a, e, r);        # a frond s long at a degrees, emerging at phase e of its year, its sheath round a crownshaft r in radius
module Pc(k, m, s, e);        # pieces k to k + m - 1 of a frond's rachis
module Pp(k, s, e);           # a drawn piece of a rachis
module Ln(k, s, e, h);        # a pair of leaflets h of a piece along piece k
module Lq(z, w, q);           # a pair of leaflets z long, raised w (-1 to 1) times leaflet_scatter, folded q toward the rachis
module Ls(z, q);              # a pair of spines z long, folded q toward the rachis
module Pw(a, q);              # half a coiled piece: two segments q long, each turned a degrees
module Lc(z, c);              # a pair of leaflets z long in a crozier, c coiled
module Lk(q, r);              # a rolled leaflet, its first piece q long and r thick
module Tk(l, r);              # a segment of trunk l long, r in radius as it formed
module Cw(r);                 # a flush's ring of scale leaves round a trunk r in radius
module Ct(z, r);              # a scale leaf z long on a trunk r in radius as it formed
module Ry(j, m, s, e);        # rays j to j + m - 1 of a fan
module Rs(j, l, u);           # ray j, l long, of a fan opened the share u
module Fl(k, n, l, u);        # thread k of a segment's n along its free part, l long, opened u
module Wg(i, m, s, e);        # palman wedges i to i + m - 1 of a fan
module Pd(n, s, e);           # a frond s long's petiole, with n pairs of teeth
module Tq(i, m, n, s);        # petiole teeth i to i + m - 1 of n, on a frond s long
module Tt(z);                 # a pair of teeth z long, one each margin
module Pn(z, q);              # a pinna z long, folded q toward the rachis
module Pl(i, m, z);           # leaflet pairs i to i + m - 1 along a pinna z long
module Ds(s, r);              # where a frond s long will hang dead, on a trunk r in radius
module Dh(s, r);              # a dead frond hanging, on a trunk r in radius
module Bs(r);                 # where a frond's boot will stand, on a trunk r in radius
module Bt(r, v);              # a boot v of its length, on a trunk r in radius
module Rq(i, m);              # roots i to i + m - 1 of this year's
module Ro(z, q);              # a root leaving the trunk z up, q of the way to the ground
module Cn(k, r);              # this year's cones, k 1 in a coning year, round a crown r in radius
module Cf(z);                 # a seed cone z long, on its stalk
module Cm(z);                 # a pollen cone z long, on its stalk
module Sp(z);                 # a megasporophyll z long
module Ov(i, m, z, q);        # ovules i to i + m - 1 along a sporophyll z long, q of its life behind it
module Ck(z, q);              # a seed cone z long, q of its life behind it
module Sd(i, m, z);           # seeds i to i + m - 1 showing on a cone z long breaking up
module Sb(n);                 # the plant's foot: suckers sent out so far
module Sk(n);                 # a sucker on its runner, the n-th
module Sh(z, r);              # a sheath card of the crownshaft, z long, round a core r in radius
module Sc(z);                 # the crownshaft's core, z long

organ leaflet leaf as leaflet area leaflet_area;
organ ray leaf as piece area ray_area;
organ dead leaf as piece area dead_area;
organ boot leaf as piece area boot_area;
organ cone cone area cone_area;
organ sheath leaf as piece area sheath_area;
organ cataphyll leaf as scale_leaf area cataphyll_area;
organ spine leaf as spine area spine_area;
organ sporophyll leaf as megasporophyll area sporophyll_area;
organ seed fruit as seed area seed_area;
organ cone_male cone area male_cone_area;
organ cone_open cone area open_cone_area;
organ palman leaf as piece area palman_area;
organ filament leaf as piece area filament_area;
organ tooth leaf as spine area tooth_area;
organ pinnule leaf as leaflet area pinnule_area;

structure frond leaf;         # one frond: its sheath, petiole, rachis, leaflets, pinnae or rays and palman, then its dead frond and boot

axiom [ Sb(0) ] /(uniform(0, 360)) &(uniform(0, lean)) Y(exp(gauss() * vigour_spread), 0);

# The crown: the trunk's growth and this year's fronds.
# A crownshaft palm's fronds and cone stand on the shaft's core, which
# lives as long as they do; each frond's sheath clasps the core below it.
rule Y(v, n) : crownshaft > 0 ->
    T(0, 1, 0, upturn * trunk_rate * v)
    Tk(trunk_rate * v * smoothstep(0, stemless_years, age), trunk_radius
      * (1 + flare * exp(-max(0, age - stemless_years) / max(flare_years, 0.001))
         + (bulge > 0 ? bulge * exp(-pow((age - bulge_age) / max(bulge_years, 0.001), 2)) : 0)))
    [ Sc(crownshaft) Fc(0, mod(floor(age + 0.5), max(1, round(flush_interval))) == 0
                             ? max(1, round(fronds_per_year * v)) : 0, age * v, trunk_radius
          * (1 + flare * exp(-max(0, age - stemless_years) / max(flare_years, 0.001))
             + (bulge > 0 ? bulge * exp(-pow((age - bulge_age) / max(bulge_years, 0.001), 2)) : 0)),
          0, max(1, round(fronds_per_year * v)))
      Cn(age >= adult_age && mod(floor(age + 1.5), max(1, round(cone_interval))) == 0 ? 1 : 0, trunk_radius * 0.97) ] Y(v, n);
rule Y(v, n) : mod(floor(age + 0.5), max(1, round(flush_interval))) == 0 ->
    T(0, 1, 0, upturn * trunk_rate * v)
    Tk(trunk_rate * v * smoothstep(0, stemless_years, age) * (1 - clamp(emerge_spread, 0, 1)), trunk_radius
      * (1 + flare * exp(-max(0, age - stemless_years) / max(flare_years, 0.001))
         + (bulge > 0 ? bulge * exp(-pow((age - bulge_age) / max(bulge_years, 0.001), 2)) : 0)))
    [ Cw(trunk_radius) ]
    Fc(0, max(1, round(fronds_per_year * v)), age * v, trunk_radius
          * (1 + flare * exp(-max(0, age - stemless_years) / max(flare_years, 0.001))
             + (bulge > 0 ? bulge * exp(-pow((age - bulge_age) / max(bulge_years, 0.001), 2)) : 0)),
       trunk_rate * v * smoothstep(0, stemless_years, age) * clamp(emerge_spread, 0, 1)
         / max(1, round(fronds_per_year * v)), max(1, round(fronds_per_year * v)))
    Cn(age >= adult_age && mod(floor(age + 1.5), max(1, round(cone_interval))) == 0 ? 1 : 0, trunk_radius
       * (1 + flare * exp(-max(0, age - stemless_years) / max(flare_years, 0.001))
          + (bulge > 0 ? bulge * exp(-pow((age - bulge_age) / max(bulge_years, 0.001), 2)) : 0))) Y(v, n);
rule Y(v, n) ->
    T(0, 1, 0, upturn * trunk_rate * v)
    Tk(trunk_rate * v * smoothstep(0, stemless_years, age), trunk_radius
      * (1 + flare * exp(-max(0, age - stemless_years) / max(flare_years, 0.001))
         + (bulge > 0 ? bulge * exp(-pow((age - bulge_age) / max(bulge_years, 0.001), 2)) : 0))) Y(v, n);

# A segment of trunk thickens as it ages by thicken of its radius.
interpret Tk(l, r) -> !(r * (1 + thicken * (1 - exp(-age / max(thicken_years, 0.001))))) F(l);

# A flush's scale leaves: out to the trunk's surface, then up it, leaning
# out cataphyll_angle degrees, the surface moving out as the trunk
# thickens.
decompose Cw(r) : cataphylls > 0 -> ring(max(1, round(cataphylls))) [ Ct(cataphyll_length, r) ];
decompose Cw(r) -> ;
rule Ct(z, r) : age + dt >= cataphyll_life -> ;
interpret Ct(z, r) ->
    &(90) f(r * (1 + thicken * (1 - exp(-age / max(thicken_years, 0.001)))))
    ^(90 - cataphyll_angle) cataphyll(z);

# The foot sends up suckers.
rule Sb(n) : suckers > n && t >= sucker_age && rand() < sucker_rate -> [ Sk(n + 1) ] Sb(n + 1);

# This year's fronds, a little apart up the trunk, each turned on round
# the spiral.
decompose Fc(i, m, c, r, d, k) : m > 1 ->
    Fc(i, floor(m / 2), c, r, d, k) Fc(i + floor(m / 2), m - floor(m / 2), c, r, d, k);
decompose Fc(i, m, c, r, d, k) : m == 1 ->
    Tk(internode + d, crownshaft > 0 ? trunk_radius * 0.97 : r) /(divergence + gauss() * 3)
    [ Fr(frond_length * (frond_seed + (1 - frond_seed) * (1 - exp(-c / size_years)))
         * uniform(1 - frond_spread, 1 + frond_spread),
         gauss() * angle_spread, emerge_spread * (i + uniform(0, 1)) / k,
         crownshaft > 0 ? trunk_radius * 0.97 : r) ];
decompose Fc(i, m, c, r, d, k) -> ;

# A frond dies after frond_life years: it hangs from its seat beside its
# foot, or falls and leaves its boot, or falls.
rule Pc(k, m, s, e) : age + dt >= frond_life -> ;
rule Ds(s, r) : age + dt >= frond_life && skirt > 0 -> Dh(s, r);
rule Ds(s, r) : age + dt >= frond_life -> ;
interpret Ds(s, r) -> ;
rule Dh(s, r) : age + dt >= dead_life -> ;
# The boot's seat at the frond's foot, on a trunk r in radius, turns to
# the boot when the frond (and its dead frond) is gone.
rule Bs(r) : age + dt >= frond_life + (skirt > 0 ? dead_life : 0) && boots > 0 && boot_spread <= 0 -> Bt(r, 1);
rule Bs(r) : age + dt >= frond_life + (skirt > 0 ? dead_life : 0) && boots <= 0 -> ;
interpret Bs(r) -> ;
rule Bt(r, v) : age + dt >= boot_life -> ;
# The frond holds its dead frond and boot until they go: a generous bound,
# since what is gone leaves nothing within it.
rule frond : age >= frond_life + dead_life + boot_life + 4 * dt -> ;
# A dead frond hangs from the trunk's surface beside its foot, which
# formed with the frond, a little up from the frond's way out of the
# trunk, so a lying stem's dead fronds sit beside it, not in the ground.
# It stands out at its last living angle and falls over dead_fold_years
# to dead_angle from the vertical, by gravity, flat against an upright
# trunk.
interpret Dh(s, r) ->
    &(90) steer(0, 1, 0, 0.25)
    f(r * (1 + thicken * (1 - exp(-(age + frond_life) / max(thicken_years, 0.001)))))
    steer(0, -1, 0, 1)
    ^(180 - lerp(min(old_angle + hang, dead_angle), dead_angle,
                 clamp(age / max(dead_fold_years, 0.001), 0, 1))) dead(s);
# A boot starts at the trunk's surface beside the frond's foot, which
# formed with the frond, then stands boot_angle from the trunk, worn
# shorter with its age.
interpret Bt(r, v) ->
    &(90)
    f(r * (1 + thicken * (1 - exp(-(age + frond_life + (skirt > 0 ? dead_life : 0))
                                   / max(thicken_years, 0.001)))))
    ^(90 - boot_angle)
    boot(boot_length * v * (1 - boot_wear * (1 - exp(-age / max(boot_wear_years, 0.001)))));

# A frond: its petiole, then its rachis piece by piece, and a fan frond's
# rays at the end. The rays share the frond's birth, so they die with it.
decompose Fr(s, a, e, r) : crownshaft > 0 && fan > 0 ->
    frond [ ring(max(1, round(shaft_cards))) [ Sh(crownshaft, r) ] ]
    Ds(s, r) Pc(0, max(1, round(pieces)), s, e) Ry(0, max(1, round(rays)), s, e)
    Wg(0, split_depth < 1 ? max(1, round(fan_spread / max(palman_angle, 1))) : 0, s, e);
decompose Fr(s, a, e, r) : crownshaft > 0 ->
    frond [ ring(max(1, round(shaft_cards))) [ Sh(crownshaft, r) ] ]
    Ds(s, r) Pc(0, max(1, round(pieces)), s, e);
# An open sheath splits down the side away from its petiole, so it does
# not close round the stem: its wedge is drawn with the petiole.
decompose Fr(s, a, e, r) : sheath_length > 0 && sheath_wedge > 0 && fan > 0 ->
    frond Bs(r) Ds(s, r) Pc(0, max(1, round(pieces)), s, e) Ry(0, max(1, round(rays)), s, e)
    Wg(0, split_depth < 1 ? max(1, round(fan_spread / max(palman_angle, 1))) : 0, s, e);
decompose Fr(s, a, e, r) : sheath_length > 0 && sheath_wedge > 0 ->
    frond Bs(r) Ds(s, r) Pc(0, max(1, round(pieces)), s, e);
decompose Fr(s, a, e, r) : sheath_length > 0 && fan > 0 ->
    frond [ ring(max(1, round(shaft_cards))) [ Sh(sheath_length, r) ] ]
    Bs(r) Ds(s, r) Pc(0, max(1, round(pieces)), s, e) Ry(0, max(1, round(rays)), s, e)
    Wg(0, split_depth < 1 ? max(1, round(fan_spread / max(palman_angle, 1))) : 0, s, e);
decompose Fr(s, a, e, r) : sheath_length > 0 ->
    frond [ ring(max(1, round(shaft_cards))) [ Sh(sheath_length, r) ] ]
    Bs(r) Ds(s, r) Pc(0, max(1, round(pieces)), s, e);
decompose Fr(s, a, e, r) : fan > 0 ->
    frond Bs(r) Ds(s, r) Pc(0, max(1, round(pieces)), s, e) Ry(0, max(1, round(rays)), s, e)
    Wg(0, split_depth < 1 ? max(1, round(fan_spread / max(palman_angle, 1))) : 0, s, e);
decompose Fr(s, a, e, r) -> frond Bs(r) Ds(s, r) Pc(0, max(1, round(pieces)), s, e);
decompose Ry(j, m, s, e) : m > 1 ->
    Ry(j, floor(m / 2), s, e) Ry(j + floor(m / 2), m - floor(m / 2), s, e);
rule Ry(j, m, s, e) : age + dt >= frond_life -> ;
decompose Wg(i, m, s, e) : m > 1 ->
    Wg(i, floor(m / 2), s, e) Wg(i + floor(m / 2), m - floor(m / 2), s, e);
rule Wg(i, m, s, e) : age + dt >= frond_life -> ;
decompose Pc(k, m, s, e) : m > 1 ->
    Pc(k, floor(m / 2), s, e) Pc(k + floor(m / 2), m - floor(m / 2), s, e);

# The first piece opens the frond, sets its sag and draws the petiole.
# An open sheath's wedge lies along the petiole's base, from the stem's
# axis out past its surface by sheath_length.
interpret Pc(k, m, s, e) : k == 0 && sheath_length > 0 && sheath_wedge > 0 && crownshaft <= 0 ->
    &(lerp(young_angle, old_angle, 1 - exp(-max(0, age + season - e) / open_years))
      + hang * clamp((age + season - e) / max(frond_life, 0.001), 0, 1))
    [ sheath(trunk_radius * (1 + flare) + sheath_length) ]
    Pd(tooth_length > 0 ? max(1, round(s * petiole / max(tooth_spacing, 0.001))) : 0, s, e)
    !(petiole_radius * 0.6) Pp(k, s, e);
interpret Pc(k, m, s, e) : k == 0 ->
    &(lerp(young_angle, old_angle, 1 - exp(-max(0, age + season - e) / open_years))
      + hang * clamp((age + season - e) / max(frond_life, 0.001), 0, 1))
    Pd(tooth_length > 0 ? max(1, round(s * petiole / max(tooth_spacing, 0.001))) : 0, s, e)
    !(petiole_radius * 0.6) Pp(k, s, e);
interpret Pc(k, m, s, e) -> Pp(k, s, e);

# A petiole with n pairs of teeth, drawn in n + 1 pieces that share its
# droop, so it bends as one piece did; the rachis then droops as before.
interpret Pd(n, s, e) ->
    T(0, -1, 0, droop * min(max(0, age + season - e), droop_years) / (1 + n))
    rest(rest_gap) young(1 - smoothstep(1, 2, (age + season - e) / unroll))
    !(petiole_radius)
    Tq(0, n, n, s)
    F(s * petiole * (1 - (n > 0 ? pow((n - 0.5) / max(1, n), 1.8 - 0.8 * tooth_tip) : 0)))
    T(0, -1, 0, droop * min(max(0, age + season - e), droop_years));

# A petiole's teeth: the petiole drawn in pieces from its foot, a pair of
# teeth at the end of each, tooth i of n the share
# x = ((i + 0.5) / n)^(1.8 - 0.8 tooth_tip) of the way from the foot,
# so teeth crowd toward the foot where they shrink toward the blade,
# tooth_length lerp(1, tooth_tip, x) long on a full-size frond.
# Eight at a time, so a petiole's many teeth nest only a few deep.
interpret Tq(i, m, n, s) : m > 1 ->
    Tq(i, ceil(m / 8), n, s)
    Tq(i + 1 * ceil(m / 8), clamp(m - 1 * ceil(m / 8), 0, ceil(m / 8)), n, s)
    Tq(i + 2 * ceil(m / 8), clamp(m - 2 * ceil(m / 8), 0, ceil(m / 8)), n, s)
    Tq(i + 3 * ceil(m / 8), clamp(m - 3 * ceil(m / 8), 0, ceil(m / 8)), n, s)
    Tq(i + 4 * ceil(m / 8), clamp(m - 4 * ceil(m / 8), 0, ceil(m / 8)), n, s)
    Tq(i + 5 * ceil(m / 8), clamp(m - 5 * ceil(m / 8), 0, ceil(m / 8)), n, s)
    Tq(i + 6 * ceil(m / 8), clamp(m - 6 * ceil(m / 8), 0, ceil(m / 8)), n, s)
    Tq(i + 7 * ceil(m / 8), clamp(m - 7 * ceil(m / 8), 0, ceil(m / 8)), n, s);
interpret Tq(i, m, n, s) : m == 1 ->
    F(s * petiole * (pow((i + 0.5) / n, 1.8 - 0.8 * tooth_tip)
                     - (i > 0 ? pow((i - 0.5) / n, 1.8 - 0.8 * tooth_tip) : 0)))
    Tt(tooth_length * min(1, s / max(frond_length, 0.001))
       * lerp(1, tooth_tip, pow((i + 0.5) / n, 1.8 - 0.8 * tooth_tip)));
interpret Tq(i, m, n, s) -> ;
# A pair of teeth, one on each margin, each tooth_angle from the way to
# the blade (over 90, leaning back toward the stem).
interpret Tt(z) ->
    [ +(90) f(petiole_radius) +(tooth_angle - 90) tooth(z) ]
    [ -(90) f(petiole_radius) -(tooth_angle - 90) tooth(z) ];

# A piece of a fan frond's costa, its last piece the fan; a piece of a
# feather frond with two pairs of leaflets. A piece beyond the unrolled
# share coils back.
interpret Pp(k, s, e) : fan > 0 -> F(s * costa / max(1, pieces));
# A circinate frond's piece of rachis (coil_turns above 0): four short
# segments, each turned toward the frond's upper face a quarter of the
# piece's share of coil_turns and shorter the nearer the tip, so the
# crozier winds inward as a tight spiral; well into the coil, no sag bends
# it. Nearer the unrolled share, or unrolled, the piece is two segments
# as below, so the frond sags as it did.
interpret Pp(k, s, e) : coil_turns > 0 && (k + 0.5) / pieces - clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1) > 0.075 ->
    T(0, -1, 0, 0)
    Pw(coil_turns * 360 / max(1, pieces) / 4
         * smoothstep(0, 0.15, (k + 0.5) / pieces - clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1)),
       s * (1 - petiole) / pieces / 4
         * lerp(1, coil_size * exp(-coil_tight * ((k + 0.5) / pieces - clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1))),
                smoothstep(0, 0.15, (k + 0.5) / pieces - clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1))))
    Ln(k, s, e, 0)
    Pw(coil_turns * 360 / max(1, pieces) / 4
         * smoothstep(0, 0.15, (k + 0.5) / pieces - clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1)),
       s * (1 - petiole) / pieces / 4
         * lerp(1, coil_size * exp(-coil_tight * ((k + 0.5) / pieces - clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1))),
                smoothstep(0, 0.15, (k + 0.5) / pieces - clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1))))
    Ln(k, s, e, 0.5);
interpret Pp(k, s, e) : coil_turns > 0 ->
    ^(coil_turns * 360 / max(1, pieces) / 2
        * smoothstep(0, 0.15, (k + 0.5) / pieces - clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1)))
    F(s * (1 - petiole) / pieces / 2
        * lerp(1, coil_size * exp(-coil_tight * max(0, (k + 0.5) / pieces - clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1))),
               smoothstep(0, 0.15, (k + 0.5) / pieces - clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1))))
    Ln(k, s, e, 0)
    ^(coil_turns * 360 / max(1, pieces) / 2
        * smoothstep(0, 0.15, (k + 0.5) / pieces - clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1)))
    F(s * (1 - petiole) / pieces / 2
        * lerp(1, coil_size * exp(-coil_tight * max(0, (k + 0.5) / pieces - clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1))),
               smoothstep(0, 0.15, (k + 0.5) / pieces - clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1))))
    Ln(k, s, e, 0.5);
interpret Pw(a, q) -> ^(a) F(q) ^(a) F(q);
interpret Pp(k, s, e) ->
    ^(curl * smoothstep(0, 0.15, (k + 0.5) / pieces - clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1)))
    F(s * (1 - petiole) / pieces * 0.5
      * (1 - coil_short * smoothstep(0, 0.15, (k + 0.5) / pieces - clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1))))
    Ln(k, s, e, 0)
    F(s * (1 - petiole) / pieces * 0.5
      * (1 - coil_short * smoothstep(0, 0.15, (k + 0.5) / pieces - clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1))))
    Ln(k, s, e, 0.5);

# The lowest acanthophylls pairs of leaflets are spines (Phoenix's
# acanthophylls), longer toward the leaflets, standing as the leaflets do
# but rising neither in groups nor apart, and folded as they are while the
# frond opens.
interpret Ln(k, s, e, h) : round(2 * (k + h)) < acanthophylls ->
    Ls(spine_length * lerp(0.4, 1, (round(2 * (k + h)) + 1) / max(1, acanthophylls)),
       leaflet_fold
          * (1 - clamp(2 * clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1) - (k + h) / pieces, 0, 1)));
interpret Ls(z, q) ->
    [ +(leaflet_angle * (1 - q)) ^(leaflet_lift * (1 - q)) spine(z) ]
    [ -(leaflet_angle * (1 - q)) ^(leaflet_lift * (1 - q)) spine(z) ];

# A twice-pinnate frond's pinnae stand at pinna_pairs of its 2 pieces
# leaflet places, spread evenly along it from its lowest; the others bear
# none.
interpret Ln(k, s, e, h) : pinna_pairs > 0
    && ceil((round(2 * (k + h)) + 1) * pinna_pairs / max(1, 2 * round(pieces)))
       <= ceil(round(2 * (k + h)) * pinna_pairs / max(1, 2 * round(pieces))) -> ;

# A circinate frond's pair of leaflets, sized along the rachis: while its
# place on the rachis is coiled (c above 0), small and pressed to the
# coil, with the leaflet rolled beside it (Lc).
interpret Ln(k, s, e, h) : coil_turns > 0 ->
    Lc(s * leaflet_length
       * ((k + h) / pieces < leaflet_peak
          ? lerp(leaflet_base, 1, (k + h) / pieces / leaflet_peak)
          : lerp(1, leaflet_tip, ((k + h) / pieces - leaflet_peak) / (1 - leaflet_peak)))
       * lerp(1, coil_pinna * exp(-coil_tight * max(0, (k + 0.5) / pieces - clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1))),
              smoothstep(0, 0.15, (k + h) / pieces - clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1))),
       smoothstep(0, 0.15, (k + h) / pieces - clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1)));
# A coiled pair: each card a fifth of its size, 25 degrees off the rachis
# and 60 toward the frond's upper face, and beside it the leaflet rolled,
# a coil as long as the coiled pieces shrink it; both fade as it unrolls.
interpret Lc(z, c) : c > 0.01 ->
    [ +(lerp(leaflet_angle, 25, c)) ^(lerp(leaflet_lift, 60, c)) leaflet(z * (1 - c * 0.8)) ]
    [ -(lerp(leaflet_angle, 25, c)) ^(lerp(leaflet_lift, 60, c)) leaflet(z * (1 - c * 0.8)) ]
    [ +(lerp(leaflet_angle, 25, c)) ^(lerp(leaflet_lift, 30, c))
      Lk(z * c * coil_size / coil_pinna / 4.2, min(petiole_radius * 0.6, z * c * coil_size / coil_pinna * coil_roll)) ]
    [ -(lerp(leaflet_angle, 25, c)) ^(lerp(leaflet_lift, 30, c))
      Lk(z * c * coil_size / coil_pinna / 4.2, min(petiole_radius * 0.6, z * c * coil_size / coil_pinna * coil_roll)) ];
interpret Lc(z, c) ->
    [ +(leaflet_angle) ^(leaflet_lift) leaflet(z) ]
    [ -(leaflet_angle) ^(leaflet_lift) leaflet(z) ];
# A rolled leaflet: six pieces, each turned 60 degrees toward the frond's
# upper face and 0.85 of the last, a coil of about one turn inward,
# thinning toward its tip.
interpret Lk(q, r) ->
    !(r) F(q) ^(60) F(q * 0.85) ^(60) F(q * 0.72) ^(60) !(r * 0.8) F(q * 0.61)
    ^(60) F(q * 0.52) ^(60) !(r * 0.6) F(q * 0.44);

# A pair of leaflets, sized along the rachis, opening by its place along
# it: small and folded toward the rachis until it has opened.
interpret Ln(k, s, e, h) ->
    Lq(s * leaflet_length
       * ((k + h) / pieces < leaflet_peak
          ? lerp(leaflet_base, 1, (k + h) / pieces / leaflet_peak)
          : lerp(1, leaflet_tip, ((k + h) / pieces - leaflet_peak) / (1 - leaflet_peak)))
       * lerp(leaflet_young, 1, clamp(2 * clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1) - (k + h) / pieces, 0, 1)),
       round(leaflet_groups) > 2
          ? 1 - 2 * mod(round(2 * (k + h)), round(leaflet_groups)) / (round(leaflet_groups) - 1)
          : (h > 0 ? -1 : 1),
       leaflet_fold
          * (1 - clamp(2 * clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1) - (k + h) / pieces, 0, 1)));
interpret Lq(z, w, q) : pinna_pairs > 0 ->
    [ +(leaflet_angle * (1 - q)) ^((leaflet_lift + leaflet_scatter * w) * (1 - q)) Pn(z, q) ]
    [ -(leaflet_angle * (1 - q)) ^((leaflet_lift + leaflet_scatter * w) * (1 - q)) Pn(z, q) ];
interpret Lq(z, w, q) ->
    [ +(leaflet_angle * (1 - q)) ^((leaflet_lift + leaflet_scatter * w) * (1 - q)) leaflet(z) ]
    [ -(leaflet_angle * (1 - q)) ^((leaflet_lift + leaflet_scatter * w) * (1 - q)) leaflet(z) ];

# A pinna: a rachis of its own, pinna_leaflets pairs of leaflets along it,
# the lowest a little way up, and one at its tip; they shorten toward the
# tip and lie folded along it while the frond opens.
interpret Pn(z, q) ->
    !(petiole_radius * 0.3) Pl(0, max(1, round(pinna_leaflets)), z * (1 - 0.5 * q))
    pinnule(z * pinnule_length * 0.75);
interpret Pl(i, m, z) : m > 1 ->
    Pl(i, ceil(m / 8), z)
    Pl(i + 1 * ceil(m / 8), clamp(m - 1 * ceil(m / 8), 0, ceil(m / 8)), z)
    Pl(i + 2 * ceil(m / 8), clamp(m - 2 * ceil(m / 8), 0, ceil(m / 8)), z)
    Pl(i + 3 * ceil(m / 8), clamp(m - 3 * ceil(m / 8), 0, ceil(m / 8)), z)
    Pl(i + 4 * ceil(m / 8), clamp(m - 4 * ceil(m / 8), 0, ceil(m / 8)), z)
    Pl(i + 5 * ceil(m / 8), clamp(m - 5 * ceil(m / 8), 0, ceil(m / 8)), z)
    Pl(i + 6 * ceil(m / 8), clamp(m - 6 * ceil(m / 8), 0, ceil(m / 8)), z)
    Pl(i + 7 * ceil(m / 8), clamp(m - 7 * ceil(m / 8), 0, ceil(m / 8)), z);
interpret Pl(i, m, z) : m == 1 ->
    F(z / max(1, round(pinna_leaflets)))
    [ +(pinnule_angle) pinnule(z * pinnule_length * lerp(1, 0.75, (i + 1) / max(1, round(pinna_leaflets)))) ]
    [ -(pinnule_angle) pinnule(z * pinnule_length * lerp(1, 0.75, (i + 1) / max(1, round(pinna_leaflets)))) ];
interpret Pl(i, m, z) -> ;

# A fan's ray j of m: each from the fan's centre at the rachis' end, spread
# across the frond's plane and cupped up a little, longest in the middle;
# folded together while the frond opens.
interpret Ry(j, m, s, e) : split_depth >= 1 ->
    [ +(fan_spread * clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0.15, 1)
        * ((j + 0.5) / max(1, round(rays)) - 0.5))
      ^(fan_cup - fan_arch * 2 * abs((j + 0.5) / max(1, round(rays)) - 0.5)) ray(s * fan_length * (0.7 + 0.3 * sin(pi * (j + 0.5) / max(1, round(rays))))) ];
interpret Ry(j, m, s, e) ->
    Rs(j, s * fan_length * (0.7 + 0.3 * sin(pi * (j + 0.5) / max(1, round(rays)))),
       clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0, 1));

# A segment that parts from the palman: out to where it parts, then its
# free part, turned down tip_droop as the frond opens, with its threads:
# the first in the sinus beside it (none beyond the fan's last segment),
# the rest along its free part to its tip. A segment j bears
# floor((j + 1) filaments) - floor(j filaments) threads.
interpret Rs(j, l, u) ->
    [ +(fan_spread * max(u, 0.15) * ((j + 0.5) / max(1, round(rays)) - 0.5))
      ^(fan_cup - fan_arch * 2 * abs((j + 0.5) / max(1, round(rays)) - 0.5))
      f(l * (1 - split_depth))
      [ &(tip_droop * u) ray(l * split_depth)
        Fl(1, floor((j + 1) * filaments) - floor(j * filaments), l * split_depth, u) ]
      Fl(j < max(1, round(rays)) - 1 ? 0 : -1, floor((j + 1) * filaments) - floor(j * filaments),
         l * split_depth, u) ];
# Thread k of a segment's n: k 0 in its sinus, half a segment's turn
# aside; k from 1 along its free part, the last at its tip. Each hangs
# toward the ground.
interpret Fl(k, n, l, u) : k == 0 && n >= 1 ->
    [ +(0.5 * fan_spread * max(u, 0.15) / max(1, round(rays)))
      steer(0, -1, 0, 0.85) filament(filament_length * l * u) ];
interpret Fl(k, n, l, u) : k >= 1 && k <= n - 1 ->
    [ f(l * 0.97 * k / max(1, n - 1)) steer(0, -1, 0, 0.85) filament(filament_length * l * u) ]
    Fl(k + 1, n, l, u);
interpret Fl(k, n, l, u) -> ;

# The palman, the fan's unsplit middle: wedge i of m from its centre out
# to where its segments part, as long as the segments there are.
interpret Wg(i, m, s, e) : m == 1 ->
    [ +(fan_spread * clamp((age + season - e) / unroll, 0.15, 1)
        * ((i + 0.5) / max(1, round(fan_spread / max(palman_angle, 1))) - 0.5))
      ^(fan_cup - fan_arch * 2 * abs((i + 0.5) / max(1, round(fan_spread / max(palman_angle, 1))) - 0.5))
      palman(s * fan_length * (1 - split_depth) * 1.04
             * (0.7 + 0.3 * sin(pi * (i + 0.5) / max(1, round(fan_spread / max(palman_angle, 1)))))) ];
interpret Wg(i, m, s, e) -> ;

# A coning year's cones, at the crown's centre among the young fronds or
# in a ring among the leaf bases: a male plant's pollen cones, a female's
# crown of sporophylls round the stem's tip (in two rings, half and three
# tenths of the trunk's radius out), or its seed cones. A plant bearing
# both sexes bears the female's.
decompose Cn(k, r) : k <= 0 -> ;
decompose Cn(k, r) : dioecious >= 0.5 && sex < 0.5 && male_cone > 0 ->
    ring(max(1, round(male_cones))) [ &(90) f(r * (male_cones > 1.5 ? max(cone_offset, 0.4) : cone_offset)) ^(90) Cm(male_cone * uniform(0.92, 1.08)) ];
decompose Cn(k, r) : dioecious >= 0.5 && sex < 0.5 -> ;
decompose Cn(k, r) : sporophylls > 0 ->
    ring(ceil(sporophylls / 2)) [ &(90) f(r * 0.5) ^(90) Sp(sporophyll_length * uniform(0.9, 1.1)) ]
    [ /(180 / ceil(sporophylls / 2)) ring(floor(sporophylls / 2))
      [ &(90) f(r * 0.3) ^(90) Sp(sporophyll_length * uniform(0.85, 1)) ] ];
decompose Cn(k, r) : crown_cone > 0 ->
    ring(max(1, round(cones))) [ &(90) f(r * (cones > 1.5 ? max(cone_offset, 0.4) : cone_offset)) ^(90) Cf(crown_cone * uniform(0.92, 1.08)) ];
decompose Cn(k, r) -> ;
rule Cm(z) : age + dt >= male_cone_life -> ;
rule Cf(z) : age + dt >= cone_life -> ;
rule Sp(z) : age + dt >= cone_life -> ;

# A cone on its stalk, which bends in three equal turns to its lean and
# rests on the ground it reaches; a seed cone leans further as it ripens.
interpret Cm(z) : male_peduncle > 0 ->
    rest(rest_gap) !(peduncle_radius)
    &(male_lean / 3) F(male_peduncle / 3) &(male_lean / 3) F(male_peduncle / 3)
    &(male_lean / 3) F(male_peduncle / 3) cone_male(z);
interpret Cm(z) -> &(male_lean) cone_male(z);
interpret Cf(z) : peduncle > 0 ->
    rest(rest_gap) !(peduncle_radius)
    &(lerp(cone_lean, ripe_lean, clamp((age + season) / max(cone_life, dt), 0, 1)) / 3) F(peduncle / 3)
    &(lerp(cone_lean, ripe_lean, clamp((age + season) / max(cone_life, dt), 0, 1)) / 3) F(peduncle / 3)
    &(lerp(cone_lean, ripe_lean, clamp((age + season) / max(cone_life, dt), 0, 1)) / 3) F(peduncle / 3)
    Ck(z, clamp((age + season) / max(cone_life, dt), 0, 1));
interpret Cf(z) ->
    &(lerp(cone_lean, ripe_lean, clamp((age + season) / max(cone_life, dt), 0, 1)))
    Ck(z, clamp((age + season) / max(cone_life, dt), 0, 1));

# A seed cone stays closed over its seeds until, in the last tenth of its
# life, they swell and force its scales apart and it breaks up
# (`cone_open`), showing them.
interpret Ck(z, q) : q > 0.9 -> cone_open(z) Sd(0, max(0, round(cone_seeds)), z);
interpret Ck(z, q) -> cone(z);

# A breaking cone's seeds, scattered over its middle (their heights by
# the plastic number's sequence, so with the golden angle round the cone
# they spread over it rather than following one helix), each from just
# under its scales and pointing out and up.
interpret Sd(i, m, z) : m > 1 ->
    Sd(i, floor(m / 2), z) Sd(i + floor(m / 2), m - floor(m / 2), z);
interpret Sd(i, m, z) : m == 1 ->
    [ f(z * (0.2 + 0.6 * frac((i + 0.5) * 0.7549))) /(137.5 * i) &(90) f(cone_width * 0.4) ^(50)
      seed(seed_length) ];
interpret Sd(i, m, z) -> ;

# A sporophyll stands out from the stem's tip, turning toward its ripe
# angle as it ages, its seeds along the upper part of its stalk, each side
# in turn, swelling as they ripen.
interpret Sp(z) ->
    &(lerp(sporophyll_angle, sporophyll_ripe, clamp((age + dt) / max(cone_life, dt), 0, 1)))
    [ sporophyll(z) ] Ov(0, max(0, round(ovules)), z, clamp((age + dt) / max(cone_life, dt), 0, 1));
interpret Ov(i, m, z, q) : m > 1 ->
    Ov(i, floor(m / 2), z, q) Ov(i + floor(m / 2), m - floor(m / 2), z, q);
interpret Ov(i, m, z, q) : m == 1 ->
    [ f(z * (0.3 + 0.3 * (floor(i / 2) + 0.5) / max(1, ceil(ovules / 2)))) +(mod(i, 2) < 1 ? 30 : -30) steer(0, -1, 0, 0.6)
      seed(seed_length * lerp(0.4, 1, q)) ];
interpret Ov(i, m, z, q) -> ;

# A sheath card of the crownshaft: out to the core's surface, then down it
# from the frond's foot, lying against it and leaning out 1.2 degrees, so
# the shaft is a little wider at its foot than the trunk below. It lives
# as long as its frond.
rule Sh(z, r) : age + dt >= frond_life -> ;
interpret Sh(z, r) -> &(90) f(r) &(88.8) sheath(z);
rule Sc(z) : age + dt >= frond_life -> ;
interpret Sc(z) -> !(trunk_radius * 0.97) F(z);

# A sucker: out along a runner at the foot, then up, a palm of its own.
decompose Sk(n) ->
    /(uniform(0, 360)) &(90) f(sucker_distance * uniform(0.6, 1.4)) ^(90)
    /(uniform(0, 360)) &(uniform(0, lean)) Y(exp(gauss() * vigour_spread), n);

# Revision 15's draws stand last, so the draws above keep their sites.
# A boot's own length draw, from 1 ± boot_spread, when its frond is gone
# (the rules above leave a boot with boot_spread to this one).
rule Bs(r) : age + dt >= frond_life + (skirt > 0 ? dead_life : 0) && boots > 0 && boot_spread > 0
    -> Bt(r, uniform(1 - boot_spread, 1 + boot_spread));

# The foot puts out roots above the ground, root_rate a year from
# root_age until it has put out `roots`, each from a height in the root
# zone as high as it has risen (in a year it sends up a sucker, none).
rule Sb(n) : roots > 0 && t >= root_age && t < root_age + roots / max(root_rate, 0.001) ->
    Rq(0, max(1, round(root_rate))) Sb(n);
# Roots stand round the foot a golden angle apart in the order they come
# out; each one's draws are keyed by its place in the year's, so no two
# of a year's roots fall alike.
decompose Rq(i, m) : m > 1 -> Rq(i, floor(m / 2)) Rq(i + floor(m / 2), m - floor(m / 2));
decompose Rq(i, m) : m == 1 ->
    [ /(137.508 * (i + max(1, round(root_rate)) * floor(t - root_age))) Ro(root_zone * rand(4 * i + 1) * smoothstep(0, max(root_zone_years, 0.001), t),
                              rand(4 * i + 2) < root_reach ? 1 : 0.05 + 0.25 * rand(4 * i + 3)) ];
decompose Rq(i, m) -> ;
# A root leaves the trunk's surface z up and runs root_angle from
# straight down to the ground, or q of the way when it stopped short.
interpret Ro(z, q) ->
    f(z) &(90) f(trunk_radius * (1 + flare) * 0.9) &(90 - root_angle)
    !(root_radius) F(q * (z / cos(rad(root_angle)) + root_radius * 2));
